      "id": "qr5k5qf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Factors Responsible for Soil Formation"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Soil formation (pedogenesis) results from weathering of rocks interacted with organic matter, relief, climate, organisms, and time.",
        "India has six primary soil types: Alluvial, Black, Red, Laterite, Desert (Arid), and Forest/Mountain soils.",
        "Alluvial soil, deposited by the Indus, Ganga, and Brahmaputra river systems, makes the Northern Plains the food bowl of India.",
        "Black soil has a high moisture retention capacity and undergoes self-ploughing through deep cracks in dry seasons, rendering it ideal for cotton.",
        "Laterite soils undergo severe leaching from heavy rain and high temperatures, leaving behind iron and aluminium oxides."
      ],
      "id": "j2451fk"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Soil formation, also called pedogenesis, depends upon several interacting factors.",
      "id": "mbmqqk1"
    },
