      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Alluvial Soil – Detailed Explanation"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Alluvial soil is the most widespread and agriculturally important soil in India. It is formed by the deposition of sediments brought by rivers such as the Indus, Ganga and Brahmaputra. The fertility of this soil makes the Northern Plains the food bowl of India.",
      "id": "4dau7y0"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Khadar Soil",
        "Bhangar Soil"
      ],
