          "Age",
          "Newer alluvium",
          "Older alluvium"
        ],
        [
          "Fertility Level",
          "More fertile",
          "Less fertile"
        ],
        [
          "Key Feature",
          "Replenished by annual flooding",
          "Contains calcareous deposits (kankar)"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "8c38jcb"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Khadar is newer and more fertile due to annual flooding, whereas Bhangar is older and contains calcareous deposits known as kankar.",
      "id": "o46p8el"
    },
    {
      "id": "7jb52yo",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Black Soil – Why Ideal for Cotton?"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Black soil has high moisture retention capacity due to its clay content. During dry seasons it develops deep cracks that allow aeration, a property known as self-ploughing. These characteristics make it highly suitable for cotton cultivation.",
      "id": "18vbpe3"
    },
    {
      "id": "zsujarl",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Laterite Soil – Leaching Process"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Laterite soils form under conditions of high temperature and heavy rainfall. Intense leaching removes silica and bases, leaving iron and aluminium oxides behind. With fertilizers and irrigation, these soils become productive.",
      "id": "dbv8rni"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Khadar vs. Bhangar: Khadar is newer and more fertile due to annual flooding, whereas Bhangar is older and contains calcareous deposits (kankar).",
        "Self-Ploughing Property: Black soil develops deep cracks during dry seasons for aeration, highly favored in cotton cultivation.",
        "Leaching Process: Laterite soils are formed when intense leaching removes silica and bases, leaving rich deposits of iron and aluminium oxides."
      ],
      "id": "h8falv2"
    },
